        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">What review request automation actually does</h2>
        <p>
          Job marked done, the ask goes out. A completed job in the system the
          shop already uses fires a message from the business, asking that
          customer for a review. Nobody on the crew has to remember it after
          the truck leaves.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is the whole motion. The message does not write the review. It
          does not pick a star. It does not promise that a review will show up,
          or that a count will move. If the customer never replies, you have a
          record of the ask, not a review we invented.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is a capability inside a done-for-you build, not a product you
          subscribe to on its own. Review requests sit with lead capture, quote
          follow-up sequences, scheduling coordination, and invoice chasing,
          built on the tools you already pay for. The calendar is one layer.
          Follow-up after the job is another. Booking lives on the
          <a class="text-link" href="/booking-software/">booking software</a>
          page. The ask after the job is this page.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Why good NWA shops have thin review counts</h2>
        <p>
          A shop can do good work and still have a thin review count. The usual
          miss is timing and follow-through, not the quality of the job. The
          tech marks the work done, the next call is already waiting, and the
          ask sits with whoever was supposed to send it. A week later that
          person is not sure the message went out.
        </p>
        <p>
          This page does not publish a share of consumers who read reviews, a
          target count, or a star rating. We have not measured those figures
          on NWA accounts. We will not invent them. Thin counts, on this page,
          means the ask did not go out when the job was done. That is a
          follow-through problem. It is not a verdict on the work.
        </p>
        <p>
          Local shops already run and buy this tool stack. NWA Cooling &amp;
          Heating (Rogers) publicly advertises Housecall Pro online booking
          and SMS communication to homeowners across NWA and the Fort Smith
          River Valley. That is demand-side evidence, used once. It is not a
          client we are claiming, and it is not a review count.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">The triggers: firing the ask when the job is really done</h2>
        <p>
          The trigger is the moment the job is really done, not the moment
          someone remembers. In ServiceTitan, Housecall Pro, or Jobber, the
          job-complete event fires the ask. That is the only claim we make
          about those tools. If the shop runs a different system, the same
          rule holds: the event that means the job is finished is the event
          that sends the message. We do not ask you to switch tools to get
          the ask.
        </p>
        <p>
          "Done" is not the same hour in every shop. Some mark complete when
          the tech leaves. Some wait until the invoice is paid. Some wait
          until the customer signs off. Sending while the tech is still in
          the driveway is the wrong moment. Waiting on memory is the gap this
          build closes.
        </p>
        <p>
          Which event counts as done is decided on the assessment call, before
          anything sends. You see the wording. You see the trigger. Nothing
          goes to a customer until you sign off, the same gate we use before
          a text-back build messages anyone.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">What the customer actually receives</h2>
        <p>
          A message from your business. It names the business, says the job is
          done, and asks for a review. It includes a way to stop. It does not
          offer money, a discount, a gift, or an entry in a drawing. It does
          not tell the customer which star to pick.
        </p>
        <p>
          The wording is yours. We do not ship a script you have not read. If
          they do not reply, there is no review to invent. You can read what
          went out and the lack of a reply.
        </p>
        <p>
          The texting channel follows the same consent and opt-out discipline
          as
          <a class="text-link" href="/missed-call-text-back-northwest-arkansas/">missed call text back</a>.
          That page covers 10DLC registration and why a message about one
          event is not permission to build a promotion list. This page does
          not re-run that section. We follow it.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">The rules: consent, opt-out, and why no incentives or gating</h2>
        <p>
          This is the operating rule we build to, not a legal opinion. If your
          counsel wants a different line, we follow that.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Consent and opt-out.</strong> The ask identifies the
          business, says why you are writing, and includes a way to stop.
          Reply STOP and it ends. A finished job is not consent to drop that
          number onto a promotion list or a newsletter. Those need their own
          opt-in. The fuller texting rules live on the missed-call page. We
          do not loosen them here.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>No incentives.</strong> Platform rules are respected and
          incentivized asks are not sent. A discount for a review, a gift
          card, or any payment tied to the ask is out. We will not add one
          because a competitor does.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>No gating.</strong> We do not screen for a predicted happy
          answer and send the public link only to those people, while routing
          everyone else somewhere private. That is review gating, and this
          build does not do it. The ask goes to the customer the job was for,
          when the job is done, under the wording you signed off on. We do
          not write the review, and we do not post one in their name.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">What this does NOT do</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>It does not sell a standalone review app, a per-review subscription, or a per-review fee.</li>
          <li>It does not promise a star rating, a review count, or a ranking. We do not publish those figures because we do not have them.</li>
          <li>It does not send incentivized asks, and it does not gate who sees a public review link.</li>
          <li>It does not invent reviews or tell the customer what to write.</li>
          <li>It does not replace the calendar. That layer is <a class="text-link" href="/booking-software/">booking software</a>.</li>
          <li>It does not replace missed-call recovery. That layer is <a class="text-link" href="/missed-call-text-back-northwest-arkansas/">missed call text back</a>.</li>
          <li>It does not replace the phone being answered. That setup, for Fayetteville, is the <a class="text-link" href="/answering-service-fayetteville-ar/">answering service page</a>.</li>
          <li>It does not give legal advice. Consent and opt-out are decided carefully and stated plainly. Counsel sets a different line if you need one.</li>
          <li>It does not claim features of ServiceTitan, Housecall Pro, or Jobber beyond this: the job-complete event fires the ask.</li>
        </ul>

